WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:04.908
Welcome to The Masquerade Court!

2
00:00:04.908 --> 00:00:09.767
Let’s learn to play by watching a real game against the computer, step by step.

3
00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:13.871
It’s carnival night at the Doge’s ball in Venice.

4
00:00:13.871 --> 00:00:17.904
Every noble wears two hidden masks, and each mask has a power.

5
00:00:17.904 --> 00:00:21.895
On your turn you may claim any mask’s power, whether you wear it or not.

6
00:00:21.895 --> 00:00:23.980
But anyone can call your bluff!

7
00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:30.260
On the home page, choose your opponents: computer nobles,

8
00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:34.735
two to five players sharing one device, or online games with friends.

9
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.899
You can face up to four computer rivals. We’ll take on two.

10
00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:43.292
Pick the difficulty, from Easy to Brutal.

11
00:00:43.292 --> 00:00:48.172
A Normal rival bluffs now and then, and calls Unmask when a claim looks shaky.

12
00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:53.055
The seats, and who moves first, are drawn at random each ball.

13
00:00:53.055 --> 00:00:57.721
Down here are a trailer, the rulebook and your game history. Let’s open the ball!

14
00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:04.921
At the top: seven Favor with the Doge wins, and so does being the last noble still masked.

15
00:01:04.921 --> 00:01:06.388
There’s no clock.

16
00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:08.671
Each noble has a card.

17
00:01:08.671 --> 00:01:13.387
We’re Contessa Rosa, in green, against Lady Verde and Count Azzurro.

18
00:01:13.387 --> 00:01:15.692
Stars count Favor, out of seven.

19
00:01:15.692 --> 00:01:19.512
Below are ducats, the coins of the ball, and the masks still worn.

20
00:01:19.512 --> 00:01:21.725
Everyone starts with two ducats.

21
00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:24.209
Rivals’ masks are face down.

22
00:01:24.209 --> 00:01:29.779
The Claimed line lists every mask a noble claims, so you can spot someone claiming too many.

23
00:01:29.779 --> 00:01:32.291
And everyone holds a secret Intrigue.

24
00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:37.183
On the right are our masks, which only we can see: the Jester and the Doge.

25
00:01:37.183 --> 00:01:43.492
Our secret Intrigue is Honest Face: be wrongly accused of lying, and it’s revealed for two Favor.

26
00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:47.157
And the Gazette below records everything said and done.

27
00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:54.329
Lady Verde was drawn to move first, and claims the Duchess, for a Waltz worth one Favor.

28
00:01:54.329 --> 00:01:56.863
The banner asks us: do we believe it?

29
00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:00.913
Here’s the key rule: everyone else decides at the same time.

30
00:02:00.913 --> 00:02:05.459
Nobody can see who has let it pass, so every rival just shows Deciding.

31
00:02:05.459 --> 00:02:08.440
And the first to call Unmask is the challenger.

32
00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:15.085
A computer noble who wants to call Unmask waits a moment first, so a quick player can beat them to it.

33
00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:20.279
The counting aid helps: there are three of each mask, and we can see none of the Duchesses.

34
00:02:20.279 --> 00:02:25.357
Calling Unmask on an honest noble costs two Favor, or a mask if you have less.

35
00:02:25.357 --> 00:02:26.696
Let it pass.

36
00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:34.617
Then Count Azzurro cut in: he paid the band one ducat and claimed the Duchess, so the waltz wins nothing.

37
00:02:34.617 --> 00:02:39.860
Only the noble who was blocked may challenge a block, and Lady Verde called Unmask.

38
00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:42.783
But he really wore the Duchess!

39
00:02:42.783 --> 00:02:47.413
He shuffled it back into the wardrobe and drew a fresh mask, and Lady Verde,

40
00:02:47.413 --> 00:02:50.011
with no Favor to pay, lost a mask.

41
00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:55.473
Now Count Azzurro claims the Spy, to peek at one of our masks and take a ducat.

42
00:02:55.473 --> 00:02:58.412
Nobody can block the Spy. Let it pass.

43
00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:02.017
Our move. A green tag means you wear that mask.

44
00:03:02.017 --> 00:03:05.063
Bluff means you don’t, but you may claim it anyway.

45
00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:12.183
The Doge takes three ducats. The Duchess waltzes for a Favor. The Jester steals a Favor.

46
00:03:12.183 --> 00:03:18.669
The Poisoner pays four to make a noble lose a mask, and the Plague Doctor pays four to replace a lost one.

47
00:03:18.669 --> 00:03:24.104
The Gondolier swaps a mask with a noble, and the Spy peeks at one, and takes a ducat.

48
00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:26.815
Some masks block.

49
00:03:26.815 --> 00:03:33.024
The Doge stops the Jester, the Plague Doctor stops the Poisoner, and the Spy stops the Gondolier.

50
00:03:33.024 --> 00:03:37.627
And any rival may pay a ducat and claim the Duchess, to cut in on a Waltz.

51
00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:44.425
Pair powers claim two masks at once, for a stronger power, like the Royal Court’s waltz, worth two Favor.

52
00:03:44.425 --> 00:03:47.513
A pair is only honest if you wear both masks.

53
00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:53.105
Or simply: Mingle for a ducat, pay three to Toast the Doge for a Favor,

54
00:03:53.105 --> 00:03:57.565
pay four to Whisper, or seven for a scandal that costs a noble a mask.

55
00:03:57.565 --> 00:04:00.035
Nobody can challenge or block these.

56
00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:05.704
We really wear the Doge, so claiming it is honest. Three ducats, please!

57
00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:12.008
Both rivals let it pass, so we take three ducats, and the Doge goes on our Claimed line.

58
00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:18.127
Lady Verde waltzes again. Her Duchess claims are piling up, so she probably wears one.

59
00:04:18.127 --> 00:04:19.465
Let it pass.

60
00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:21.961
Now any rival may cut in.

61
00:04:21.961 --> 00:04:26.528
Count Azzurro didn’t, so it’s our turn: pay one ducat and claim the Duchess.

62
00:04:26.528 --> 00:04:29.765
We don’t wear her, so it would be a bluff. Let them dance.

63
00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:35.949
Lady Verde wins a Favor. Count Azzurro spies on her, and we let that pass too.

64
00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:38.247
We have no Favor yet.

65
00:04:38.247 --> 00:04:42.423
We don’t wear the Duchess, but let’s claim her Waltz anyway: a bluff!

66
00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:48.321
The game checks first: if someone calls Unmask, we lose a mask and the waltz fails.

67
00:04:48.321 --> 00:04:49.489
Bluff it!

68
00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:52.948
Count Azzurro calls Unmask!

69
00:04:52.948 --> 00:04:57.999
No Duchess: our bluff is exposed, the waltz fails, and we must lose a mask.

70
00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:03.393
Had we been honest, we’d shuffle the Duchess back into the wardrobe and draw a fresh mask,

71
00:05:03.393 --> 00:05:06.567
the waltz would go ahead, and he’d pay for the insult.

72
00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:12.419
We choose which mask to lose. It’s turned face up, out of play for good. We keep the Doge.

73
00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:16.171
Down to one mask: lose it, and we leave the ball.

74
00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:23.123
Lady Verde waltzes again, Count Azzurro spies on her again, and we let both pass.

75
00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:30.244
Lady Verde has two Favor. Let’s bluff the Spy, and peek at one of her masks.

76
00:05:30.244 --> 00:05:33.289
Powers aimed at a noble ask us to choose one.

77
00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:44.200
Nobody called Unmask. Our Spy saw her Duchess, and we took a ducat.

78
00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:51.779
So when she waltzes again, we know it’s real. We let her dance.

79
00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:56.260
Count Azzurro had peeked at Lady Verde twice.

80
00:05:56.260 --> 00:06:00.229
He paid four ducats to whisper that she wears the Duchess. True!

81
00:06:00.229 --> 00:06:03.403
Her last mask was torn off, and she left the ball.

82
00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:08.368
Before our move, a quick look at the rulebook, behind the Rules button.

83
00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:15.152
It has chapters on your turn, the seven masks and what each one blocks, pairs, and whispers.

84
00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:23.619
Then Unmask, blocks and cutting in, all ten secret Intrigues, and how to win.

85
00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:30.505
We could whisper too: choose a noble, then name a mask.

86
00:06:30.505 --> 00:06:34.425
Each choice shows how often they’ve claimed it, and how many we’ve seen.

87
00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:48.169
But we haven’t seen any of Count Azzurro’s masks, and with no Favor, a wrong guess costs our last mask.

88
00:06:48.169 --> 00:06:49.487
Too risky.

89
00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:55.285
Instead, we toast the Doge: three ducats for a Favor that nobody can stop.

90
00:06:56.040 --> 00:06:59.391
Count Azzurro claims the Jester, to steal that Favor.

91
00:06:59.391 --> 00:07:02.827
With only two of us left, we’re the only one who can answer.

92
00:07:02.827 --> 00:07:04.997
Let it pass: we can do better.

93
00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:09.800
The Doge blocks the Jester, and we really wear the Doge. Block!

94
00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:15.533
Count Azzurro calls Unmask on our block. But we’re honest!

95
00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:19.825
We shuffle the Doge back into the wardrobe and draw a fresh mask.

96
00:07:19.825 --> 00:07:24.833
Count Azzurro has no Favor, so he pays with a mask, and the Rumour is blocked.

97
00:07:25.480 --> 00:07:30.004
And being wrongly accused is exactly our secret Intrigue, Honest Face.

98
00:07:30.004 --> 00:07:32.132
It’s revealed, for two Favor!

99
00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:37.680
Our fresh mask is the Poisoner. Another toast: four Favor.

100
00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:46.327
Count Azzurro claims the Royal Court: the Duchess and the Doge together, for two Favor.

101
00:07:46.327 --> 00:07:51.847
But look: he has just one mask left, and a pair needs two. He can’t wear both. Unmask!

102
00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:56.852
A bluff! Count Azzurro loses his last mask and leaves the ball.

103
00:07:56.852 --> 00:07:59.769
We’re the last noble still masked, so we win!

104
00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:05.055
At the end every mask is revealed, with each noble’s Favor and secret Intrigue.

105
00:08:05.055 --> 00:08:07.275
Lady Verde wore two Duchesses!

106
00:08:07.275 --> 00:08:13.755
Her Truth Seeker Intrigue needed her to catch a liar, but she called Unmask on an honest noble instead.

107
00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:18.257
And Count Azzurro’s Intrigue was Perfect Match: use a pair power.

108
00:08:18.257 --> 00:08:20.577
That’s why he risked the Royal Court.

109
00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:25.435
From here, look back at the ballroom, play again, or see your game history.

110
00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:30.209
Remember: count the masks you can see before you call Unmask,

111
00:08:30.209 --> 00:08:34.179
bluff when the odds are on your side, and watch your rivals’ Intrigues.

112
00:08:34.179 --> 00:08:35.731
Good luck at the ball!

